1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,010
So much slop, so little time.

2
00:00:02,310 --> 00:00:04,470
This is Higher Ed Hot
Takes, and I'm Joel Goodman.

3
00:00:19,027 --> 00:00:22,777
As a web designer and marketer,
I've seen it all and unfortunately

4
00:00:22,837 --> 00:00:24,307
it all looks the same.

5
00:00:24,667 --> 00:00:26,257
But you've heard this though, right?

6
00:00:26,489 --> 00:00:31,679
I've been in the higher ed web marketing
game since 2009, and then as now we were

7
00:00:31,679 --> 00:00:35,489
talking about how everyone's website
looks the same or how every institution

8
00:00:35,489 --> 00:00:37,589
has some version of the same tagline.

9
00:00:38,219 --> 00:00:41,879
One of my favorite videos is of my good
friend Jeff Stevens doing a dramatic

10
00:00:41,879 --> 00:00:46,139
reading of dozens of university taglines
to underscore how similar they all are.

11
00:00:46,619 --> 00:00:50,339
So what happens now that we
have generative AI and LLMs?

12
00:00:50,999 --> 00:00:55,169
A lot of us have been integrating chat
GPT into our work, having it draft emails

13
00:00:55,169 --> 00:00:57,239
or website content or news stories.

14
00:00:57,539 --> 00:01:01,229
And that's not necessarily bad if you
have talented writers and editors who

15
00:01:01,229 --> 00:01:02,969
go through and revise those drafts.

16
00:01:03,389 --> 00:01:07,169
But I suspect that not many
of us do, or not many of us

17
00:01:07,169 --> 00:01:08,639
wanna wait around for an edit.

18
00:01:09,269 --> 00:01:13,379
And when budget pressures come around, as
they always do in higher ed, we turn to

19
00:01:13,379 --> 00:01:15,419
cutting staff and underfunding marketing.

20
00:01:16,034 --> 00:01:18,884
Especially now with the promise
of automation through ai.

21
00:01:19,214 --> 00:01:21,764
Why can't we just replace
that writer with chat GPT?

22
00:01:21,854 --> 00:01:22,964
It seems good enough.

23
00:01:23,414 --> 00:01:25,724
Or how about decreasing your
frontline admission staff because

24
00:01:25,724 --> 00:01:27,254
you can replace them with a chat bot.

25
00:01:27,764 --> 00:01:31,514
Even creepier, replace them with
multimodal AI that synthesizes

26
00:01:31,514 --> 00:01:33,284
voices and can talk on the phone.

27
00:01:33,764 --> 00:01:34,814
That stuff creeps me out.

28
00:01:35,474 --> 00:01:39,644
The hitch is that the stuff that we
have right now is not actually ai.

29
00:01:39,959 --> 00:01:41,219
It's machine learning.

30
00:01:41,549 --> 00:01:45,419
We've had machine learning for 20 to
30 years available to us in higher

31
00:01:45,419 --> 00:01:49,169
ed and plenty more accessible ML
tools have been in our toolbox for

32
00:01:49,169 --> 00:01:50,939
the past 10 years or so already.

33
00:01:51,449 --> 00:01:55,169
It's a fact that generative ai, all
of which are trained on the same

34
00:01:55,169 --> 00:01:59,789
core data, can't actually understand
anything because they don't think.

35
00:02:00,299 --> 00:02:02,339
LLMs are predictive algorithms.

36
00:02:02,339 --> 00:02:05,249
They guess what word goes
next to another word.

37
00:02:05,423 --> 00:02:08,543
I haven't seen any proof that it
knows how to differentiate, say,

38
00:02:08,723 --> 00:02:12,503
between Wheaton College and Illinois
and Wheaton College in Massachusetts.

39
00:02:12,833 --> 00:02:16,013
At least not to a degree that ensures
you're not giving prospective students

40
00:02:16,013 --> 00:02:19,733
incorrect information or information
that contradicts your values and brand.

41
00:02:20,453 --> 00:02:24,113
LLMs are at a minimum, a
decade away from anything like

42
00:02:24,113 --> 00:02:25,463
real thought and interaction.

43
00:02:25,943 --> 00:02:26,603
Right now.

44
00:02:26,603 --> 00:02:30,173
Their responses are purely predictive,
and while they might trick you into

45
00:02:30,173 --> 00:02:34,553
believing they know things at times,
we've all experienced the hallucinations.

46
00:02:35,063 --> 00:02:39,023
It's giving too much credit to say they
make mistakes, because these algorithms

47
00:02:39,023 --> 00:02:41,633
have no actual thought or knowledge.

48
00:02:41,933 --> 00:02:43,433
They don't make mistakes.

49
00:02:43,673 --> 00:02:46,223
They just select inaccurate
information to serve up.

50
00:02:46,588 --> 00:02:50,578
Like, I just think there are way too many
questions around the ethics of that for us

51
00:02:50,578 --> 00:02:53,248
to blindly be pouring cash into new tech.

52
00:02:53,908 --> 00:02:57,328
But this comes down to an extremely
nihilistic outlook on society.

53
00:02:57,658 --> 00:03:02,338
A lot of the uses for AI being sold
to us across every sector are just

54
00:03:02,338 --> 00:03:04,348
cynicism wrapped up in a sales pitch.

55
00:03:04,768 --> 00:03:08,188
When you outsource your hiring
to an LLM, what sort of respect

56
00:03:08,188 --> 00:03:09,598
are you showing your applicants?

57
00:03:09,801 --> 00:03:12,951
How would you feel if you found out your
new boss didn't value your expertise

58
00:03:12,951 --> 00:03:14,451
enough to write an acceptance letter?

59
00:03:14,731 --> 00:03:17,911
When putting an AI powered voice
bot between your admission staff

60
00:03:17,911 --> 00:03:21,301
and prospective students, do you
feel you're showing real care?

61
00:03:21,721 --> 00:03:23,521
These are the things
that keep me up at night.

62
00:03:23,911 --> 00:03:27,121
Because we're losing authenticity
and we're losing connection,

63
00:03:27,271 --> 00:03:31,111
and we're taking humanity out
of inherently human experiences.

64
00:03:31,351 --> 00:03:34,621
And when you take the humanity out of
the connection with your prospective

65
00:03:34,621 --> 00:03:37,321
students, you lose the only edge you have.

66
00:03:37,711 --> 00:03:41,521
There aren't that many differentiators
in the marketing that higher ed does.

67
00:03:41,821 --> 00:03:45,301
So that when you lose that core
one, your people and your culture

68
00:03:45,301 --> 00:03:48,931
and the ethos behind what you
do and give it to an algorithm

69
00:03:48,931 --> 00:03:51,691
trained on generic slop, you lose.

70
00:03:52,141 --> 00:03:53,701
And so do your prospects.

71
00:03:54,401 --> 00:03:58,151
Your people are the one selling
proposition that you have

72
00:03:58,151 --> 00:04:00,161
over any other institution.

73
00:04:00,434 --> 00:04:03,614
If you're shoveling your prospective
students off to talk to a robot,

74
00:04:04,034 --> 00:04:08,684
essentially there's a question about how
much you care about them and the process.

75
00:04:09,074 --> 00:04:12,734
How much do you really trust a
large language model or a generative

76
00:04:12,734 --> 00:04:15,889
model to know your brand and to
accurately express your values?

77
00:04:16,869 --> 00:04:21,044
I'm not saying this level of disrespect
is intentional because most of

78
00:04:21,044 --> 00:04:22,484
us in higher ed are good people.

79
00:04:23,159 --> 00:04:27,329
But the level of disrespect that's born
out of a lack of serious consideration

80
00:04:27,329 --> 00:04:29,249
to these things is noticeable.

81
00:04:29,699 --> 00:04:33,179
If the quality of your content declines
because you didn't care enough to put

82
00:04:33,179 --> 00:04:37,319
an actual talented person on the job,
you're disrespecting your audiences.

83
00:04:37,714 --> 00:04:41,284
When your Gen AI images start looking
like everyone else's, you're gonna lose

84
00:04:41,284 --> 00:04:45,064
market share and brand standing while
infringing on the work of real artists.

85
00:04:45,754 --> 00:04:47,644
Slop begets slop.

86
00:04:48,094 --> 00:04:50,734
And a resurgence of
craft is the only remedy.

87
00:04:51,244 --> 00:04:54,694
The web is now filled with AI
generated slop, and the race to

88
00:04:54,694 --> 00:04:57,154
hegemonic mediocrity is full sprint.

89
00:04:57,784 --> 00:05:01,984
Higher ed already has a serious trust
issue, and that's only going to get

90
00:05:01,984 --> 00:05:05,974
worse if we don't remember that humanity
is at the heart of the work that we do.

91
00:05:06,664 --> 00:05:11,374
Look, LLMs have real uses and those
are largely the same as what we've been

92
00:05:11,374 --> 00:05:13,564
using machine learning for for years.

93
00:05:13,984 --> 00:05:15,394
So think a bit broader.

94
00:05:15,754 --> 00:05:20,834
Generative AI, the stuff ChatGPT, Claude
and Mid Journey do is the lowest common

95
00:05:20,854 --> 00:05:23,284
denominator and the easiest thing to sell.

96
00:05:23,524 --> 00:05:27,304
It's surface level stuff and
honestly the stuff a real person

97
00:05:27,304 --> 00:05:28,684
should be doing in production.

98
00:05:29,284 --> 00:05:32,824
You should be using LLMs to support
your craft and not replace it.

99
00:05:33,244 --> 00:05:34,594
That's the lesson here.

100
00:05:34,984 --> 00:05:37,384
Use them for research or data analysis.

101
00:05:37,384 --> 00:05:39,574
Use them for finding
patterns or connecting your

102
00:05:39,574 --> 00:05:40,894
different databases together.

103
00:05:41,134 --> 00:05:44,854
Use them to manage data integrity or
maybe like keep your notes organized,

104
00:05:45,124 --> 00:05:47,464
but don't outsource your human touch.

105
00:05:47,734 --> 00:05:50,014
The world doesn't need more slop.

106
00:05:50,494 --> 00:05:51,634
You're better than that.

107
00:05:53,224 --> 00:05:53,969
Thanks for listening.

108
00:05:53,989 --> 00:05:57,124
If you want to connect, find me Joel
Goodman on Blue Sky and LinkedIn.

